      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/112ee04f02087fbf9f477a4cc002a7b24f7881e1ac2e37a1566ccc43e948cf0a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$",
      "worked_solution": "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{4}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\pi }=2.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $2$ unit down. Hence, $D=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=2+(-2)=0.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)-2.\n$$",
