    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65550",
      "knowledge_point_id": "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/c904a1eb628e726647a6f655a567a0939f7b7d404f0b32c6c4b4adaeca847916.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B-D?$",
      "worked_solution": "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units down. Hence, $D=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $B-D=(\\frac{5}{4})-(-3)=\\frac{5}{4}+3=\\frac{17}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5x}{4})-3.\n$$",
