    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-143665",
      "knowledge_point_id": "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/39d3bd73ac0f60f21cc9e71cbf46d62901fcc07cab3b06cd84a496b1e1415a1a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$",
      "worked_solution": "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $5$ units down. Hence, $D=-5.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{2}+(-5)=-\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})-5.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
