      "worked_solution": "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4(x+\\frac{C}{4}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{4}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4(x+\\frac{C}{4}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/4c5147d95cc4d5df1d847f5fe9f580d9595d0f4d8c2c8209efa1f2a3cd9c5aea.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x)$ by $\\frac{9\\pi }{40}-\\frac{\\pi }{8}=\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{4}=-\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\n$$",
